<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Patch>

  <!-- Moves the head-height half of the fix out of C# entirely.

       attachedToHead makes MoteAttached.TimeInterval replace the mote's offset
       with a live BaseHeadOffsetAt call every tick. That method carries Big and
       Small's postfix, so the head offset scales with the pawn continuously and
       can never go stale - which a creation-time C# correction could.

       It stacks on top of the offset Glowing eyes genes already passes, whose
       0.27 is the vanilla head height (0.34) less the eye's own 0.07 drop. So
       unattachedDrawOffset takes that 0.34 back off. Despite its name, Mote.DrawPos
       applies unattachedDrawOffset unconditionally, attached or not; graphicData
       drawOffset would NOT work here, because Graphic_Mote.DrawWorker ignores the
       loc it is handed and draws at mote.DrawPos.

       Net result on a pawn nothing is resizing: 0.27 + 0.34 - 0.34 = 0.27, exactly
       where it was. On a resized pawn the 0.34 becomes 0.34 x the head multiplier.

       Patched on the abstract parent because PatchOperations run on raw XML before
       ParentName inheritance is resolved, so the mote block only exists there. -->

  <Operation Class="PatchOperationFindMod">
    <mods>
      <li>Glowing eyes genes</li>
    </mods>
    <match Class="PatchOperationSequence">
      <operations>

        <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[@Name="EyeGlowMote"]/mote</xpath>
          <value>
            <attachedToHead>true</attachedToHead>
            <unattachedDrawOffset>(0, 0, -0.34)</unattachedDrawOffset>
          </value>
        </li>

        <!-- A soft fade instead of an instant pop.

             Mote.TimeInterval destroys a needsMaintenance mote once
             TicksGame - lastMaintainTick exceeds fadeOutTime.SecondsToTicks(), and
             at 0 that is a single tick. This is no longer needed for throttling -
             the throttle calls Maintain on the motes itself on skipped ticks - so
             the only thing this buys now is that the glow fades over half a second
             when it should stop, rather than vanishing between two frames. That
             matters most when the speed gate closes.

             Cost of keeping it: GEG destroys the right eye's mote explicitly on a
             rotation change, but the left eye's is only ever dropped by going
             unmaintained, so turning away from south leaves it fading at the
             south-facing offset for this long. Set to 0 to trade the fade back for
             no tail on turns. Nothing changes while the glow is maintained. -->
        <li Class="PatchOperationReplace">
          <xpath>/Defs/ThingDef[@Name="EyeGlowMote"]/mote/fadeOutTime</xpath>
          <value>
            <fadeOutTime>0.5</fadeOutTime>
          </value>
        </li>

      </operations>
    </match>
  </Operation>

</Patch>
